course: religion-ethics
lesson: 83
title: White lies: the ugly jumper and the bad drawing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A white lie is a small lie told to be nice, like saying "I love it!" about a present you secretly think is awful.
explain: Picture Gran handing Ruby a bright orange jumper with a purple llama: "D'you love it, pet?" Ruby thinks it's hideous, but she can truly say, "You made this for me? Thank you, Gran!"
explain: The trick is to look for the true kind thing: thank people for their love, effort and time, and praise what you really do like, so you rarely need the lie.
explain: Christians are asked to speak "the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15): honest words said gently, at the right time, for the good of the listener.
explain: Thinkers disagree about white lies: Kant said even small lies are wrong, others say a tiny lie that only spares feelings can be fine, and many warn that small lies add up.
fact: A white lie is a small lie told to be nice or to spare someone's feelings; philosophers disagree whether they are harmless.
fact: The Bible asks us to speak "the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15).
fact: The thinker Kant said even small lies are wrong; others think a tiny lie that only spares feelings can be fine, but that small lies add up.
fact: An old Jewish teaching says every bride should be praised on her wedding day as beautiful.
fact: Christians are told to "encourage one another and build each other up" (1 Thessalonians 5:11), and Jesus welcomed little children (Mark 10:14).
fact: Teachers find that praising effort and ideas ("tell me about it") helps children more than empty praise ("you're a genius").
fact: If something is done and cannot change, find something true and nice to say; if someone is asking before deciding, be gently honest.
fact: Proverbs 27:6: "Wounds from a friend can be trusted." Aristotle said the virtue lies in the middle: not a flatterer and not a brute.
fact: Christians are asked to "give thanks in all circumstances" (1 Thessalonians 5:18): gratitude is owed for the giving, whatever the gift.
fact: When someone sincerely asks for honest feedback, kind, polite honesty helps them; "An honest witness tells the truth" (Proverbs 12:17).
fact: With someone who is ill, be both honest and loving; the big medical truths are for grown-ups to share.
fact: Some philosophers say that if everyone understands "lovely dinner" as "thank you", it may not be a lie at all; "Let your conversation be always full of grace" (Colossians 4:6).
fact: Timing matters: "There is a time to be silent and a time to speak" (Ecclesiastes 3:7); the Buddha taught to say what is true and helpful at the right time.
fact: Slang check: "mid" means average, nothing special; "boss" in Liverpool means great; "wicked" in Boston means very; "pet" in the North of England is a kind word like "love".
exercise: What is a white lie? | A small lie told to be nice or to spare feelings | A lie told on a snowy day in winter | A lie told only to teachers at school | A big lie that gets someone else in trouble
exercise: Gran asks Ruby if she loves the llama jumper she knitted. Which reply is both true and kind? | You made this for me? Thank you, Gran! | I love it, it's the best jumper in the world! | It's hideous, Gran, I'm never wearing it! | Can I have the money instead next time?
exercise: Isla, 5, shows a purple blob drawing: "It's you! Is it good?" What is the best answer? | I love the purple, tell me about it! | It's rubbish, you can't even draw legs! | It's the best drawing anyone has ever done! | Go away, I'm busy right now
exercise: Why is "tell me about it" better than "you're a genius" for a little child? | Praising effort and ideas helps children more than empty praise | Because little children cannot understand the word genius | Because it is always wrong to praise a child at all | Because the drawing was really very bad indeed
exercise: Mum asks Kofi if he likes her new haircut. It's done already. What is wise? | Find something true and nice to say, since it can't change now | Tell her it looks terrible, so that she knows the truth and can do better next time | Say nothing at all and walk out of the room | Tell her to get it cut again tomorrow
exercise: Nan in Liverpool asks, "Boss, innit? Best you've had?" What does "boss" mean? | Great | Salty | Bossy | Big
exercise: Nan's scouse is a bit salty. If she asks for real advice, what should Jack say? | Maybe a bit less salt next time, Nan | It's perfect, don't change a single thing | It's horrible and I'm not eating any more | Ask somebody else, I don't know about food
exercise: Rhys's mate asks, "Am I rubbish at footy?" He kind of is. What is honest and helpful? | Not the fastest, but your passing's getting better. Want to practise? | Yeah, you're rubbish, you should quit football now and try a different sport instead | No, you're the best player in the whole school | Dunno, I never watch you play anyway
exercise: Auntie gives Divya a book she already has. What should Divya do? | Thank her warmly, and if asked, gently say she has it and loves it | Throw the book back and say she already has it | Pretend to be really excited about it and never mention, to anyone, that she already has a copy | Ask Auntie for money to buy a different present
exercise: Aaliyah thinks her friend's cake looks "mid". What does "mid" mean? | Average, nothing special | Delicious and very tasty indeed | Burnt black on the bottom | Huge, the size of a table
exercise: The friend made the cake herself for her birthday and asks, "Is it nice?" What is a good answer? | You made this yourself? That's so cool! | It's mid, if I'm being honest with you, babes | It's the best cake ever baked anywhere, ever | Shop cakes are much better than this one
exercise: Mrs Okafor sincerely asks, "Was that lesson boring?" Why is a white lie unhelpful here? | She wants to improve, and nobody can if everyone says "no" | Because teachers always know when children are lying to them, so there's no point in trying | Because white lies are only allowed at home | Because it was not boring at all
exercise: Grandad is ill and asks, "I look alright, don't I?" What is honest and loving? | You look tired, Grandad, but I'm so glad you're here | You look awful, Grandad, you should be in bed and not talking to us | You look perfect, nothing is wrong with you | I don't know, ask Mum
exercise: Connor says "wicked good dinnah, Ma" about dry turkey. Why might it barely be a lie? | Everyone may hear it as "thank you", not as a careful review | Because the turkey was really delicious all along | Because Connor is from Boston, and people from Boston never, ever tell lies to anyone | Because it is Thanksgiving, when lying is allowed
exercise: Five minutes before the party Grace asks, "Does this dress make me look silly?" What fits best? | Say something true like "That colour's lovely on you" and go | Tell her she looks silly in it, so that she knows for next time | Tell her to go home and change, even if she is late to the party | Laugh and say nothing to her, then tell everyone about it later
exercise: What does "speaking the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15) mean? | Being honest in a gentle way that cares about the listener | Telling the truth only to people you love | Saying loving things even when they are false | Shouting the truth so everyone can hear it
test: Your cousin says, "White lies are always fine, everyone tells them." What is the most accurate reply? | Thinkers disagree; many say small lies add up, and you can usually find a true kind thing | Everyone agrees white lies are always fine | Everyone agrees all white lies are terrible sins, and that is why the Bible lists them with the other commandments | Only grown-ups are allowed to tell white lies
test: "Gran, that jumper's minging," says your brother («minging» means «horrible, ugly»). Gran looks hurt. What did he get wrong? | He was honest without love; he could have thanked her for her work | He should have lied and said it was the very best jumper anyone had ever knitted in the world | Nothing, being rude is the same as being honest | He should have said it behind her back instead
test: The class all say "no" when Mrs Okafor asks if the lesson was boring. What would happen if nobody ever told her the truth? | She would keep teaching the same boring way and never improve | She would get much better at teaching all by herself, because teachers always know what works | The class would get a reward for being polite | She would know anyway, so nothing would change
test: A cartoon shows a kid telling Grandad, "You look awful, you're dying!" and calling it "just being honest". What is wrong? | Honesty should be gentle; the big medical truths are for grown-ups to share | Nothing, that is exactly the right way to talk to Grandad | The kid should have lied and said Grandad looked young | Honesty means you must say every thought out loud
test: Divya's sister says, "Just say you haven't read it, Auntie will never know." What is the better plan? | Thank her for the gift; if asked straight, say gently she has it and loves it | Say it's new, then sell it on the internet later | Tell Auntie that she always gives boring presents, so next time she should just give money instead | Hide the book and never mention it again
test: "Crap, Dad, these pancakes are cremated," says Jayden. Dad asks, "Out of ten?" What would be honest and kind? | "Two for looks, ten for effort, thanks Dad!" | "Ten, they're the best I've ever had, honest!" | "Zero, you can't cook anything at all, can you, Dad" | "I'm not eating them, Dad, make me some new ones"
test: Your friend asks, "Is my singing good?" before her talent show, and you can't think of anything nice. What helps? | Encourage her bravery and the bits that work; save advice for after | Tell her she's off key and should drop out tonight before she embarrasses herself | Say she's the best singer in the country | Tell everyone else she can't sing
test: A boy in Boston says "wicked hot" about the weather. What does "wicked" mean there? | Very | Evil | Strange | Cold
test: An old Jewish teaching says every bride should be praised as beautiful. What does this show? | Some traditions see kind praise on special days as right, not a harmful lie | That Jewish teaching says all lies are allowed | That brides must always be told the truth about their looks | That only rabbis are allowed to praise a bride
test: Kofi's mum asks about her haircut before she books it, showing him a photo. What should he do? | Be gently honest, since she is still deciding | Say "love it" whatever he thinks, to be polite | Say nothing, because haircuts are not his business | Tell her all her haircuts look bad
test: Why is timing important with a kind truth? | The same truth can help at one moment and only hurt at another | Because truth is only true at certain times of day, like in the morning before school starts | Because you should always wait a year before saying anything | Because timing does not matter at all
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a "white lie"? | A minor falsehood uttered with the intention of sparing the feelings of another | A grave falsehood uttered with the intention of procuring another's punishment | A truthful statement uttered with the intention of causing distress | A falsehood uttered exclusively in respect of matters of meteorology
exam: Select the response consistent with the injunction to speak "the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15) when receiving an unwanted gift. | The expression of sincere gratitude for the giver's effort and affection | The immediate declaration that the gift is wholly unsatisfactory and ought to be exchanged forthwith | The fabrication of extravagant praise irrespective of one's opinion | The refusal to acknowledge the receipt of the gift whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a falsehood is unhelpful when a teacher sincerely solicits candid feedback. | The teacher is thereby deprived of the information required for improvement | The teacher is thereby obliged to impose a penalty upon the entire class for its want of candour | The teacher is thereby rendered unable to complete the lesson | The teacher is thereby compelled to repeat the identical lesson verbatim
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the moral status of white lies is consistent with the established positions of the thinkers named in this lesson. | Kant held all falsehood impermissible, whereas other thinkers have regarded minor kindly falsehoods as acceptable | All philosophers unanimously regard white lies as wholly permissible | All philosophers unanimously regard white lies as the gravest of wrongs | No philosopher has ever expressed an opinion concerning white lies
